WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.820
The power monitoring module, or P M M, provides an interface for

2
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.060
observing and managing parts of the shelf system.

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.240
Its precise functions follow the selected specification and implementation.

4
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.660
At rack level, the important questions are what is measured, which decisions

5
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.700
are coordinated, and what happens when communication or a controller is unavailable.

6
00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.060
Telemetry can reveal a limit or fault condition, but observation is not

7
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.140
the same as protection.

8
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.760
Fast local protective behavior must not depend on an assumed software response

9
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.320
unless the qualified design explicitly establishes that architecture.

10
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.000
Likewise, a management command doesn't prove that every downstream device follows the

11
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:37.840
intended sequence.

12
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.500
Record response ownership at each timescale and boundary.

13
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:46.160
Ask how load sharing, capacity limits and recovery requests interact with local

14
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:46.700
protection.

15
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.900
Review degraded communication and stale or missing measurements as part of the

16
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.100
design, not only the nominal dashboard.

17
00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.260
The P M M specification is a supporting resource here; the course's

18
00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.600
focus remains the rack-level consequence of control and visibility, not its register

19
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:02.100
details.

